Subject: [PATCH] seastone: fancontrol-F2B: a 20 % fan floor (MINPWM/MINSTOP 51)
 and MINTEMP 35

The stock DX010 fan table (2017, pwmconfig) never lets the ten chassis
fans below PWM 89 (35 % duty, about 7,400 rpm): at MINTEMP 26 C on the
intake sensor the curve is already above its floor in any room, so the
fans run at 35 % or more all the time and the switch is loud for a home
rack. This lowers the floor to PWM 51 (20 %, about 3,900-4,300 rpm) and
starts the ramp at an intake of 35 C instead of 26, so the fans sit at
the floor in a room up to 35 C and ramp as before above it. MINSTART
(the spin-up kick), MAXTEMP, MAXPWM and the hysteresis are unchanged,
and so are thermalctld's own policies (any sensor over its high
threshold still means full speed, over critical a power cycle).

Measured on a DX010 (C0 stepping, one 100G link, room at 23-25 C): at
PWM 51 all ten fans stay OK at 3,866-4,278 rpm and the ASIC settles at
about 56 C (52 C at PWM 64, 45 C at 89; high threshold 70, critical
75). PWM 38 (15 %) was tried and rejected: the fans held, but the ASIC
climbed past 62 C without levelling.

Why the F2B file: /etc/init.d/fancontrol.sh copies fancontrol-F2B (or
-B2F, by the fan direction read from the CPLD) over fancontrol at every
boot, and pmon copies that into its /etc/fancontrol at every start, so
only the direction file persists. Editing fancontrol alone lasts until
the next reboot. The B2F table (reversed airflow units) is left stock:
nothing here was measured on one.
